<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPFN Mobile — the Android harness manifest.

  INTERNET is the only permission this file declares: the harness sends real requests to
  whatever base URL its launch names. It is NOT the only permission the installed app
  holds — the Google adapter's own dependencies merge theirs in, and a debug build's
  merged manifest carries android.permission.USE_BIOMETRIC, android.permission.USE_FINGERPRINT
  and Credential Manager's app-namespaced DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION
  alongside it. Read the merged manifest, not this file, to know what a build asks for:

    grep -o 'uses-permission android:name="[^"]*"' \
      tools/harness/android/build/intermediates/merged_manifests/debug/processDebugManifest/AndroidManifest.xml

  Cleartext used to be a blanket `usesCleartextTraffic="true"`, and it is now a network
  security configuration naming exactly one host: the server this build was configured
  with, and nothing else. "This app may speak plain HTTP to the machine the server is on"
  and "this app may speak plain HTTP to anything" are different statements, and only the
  first one is true. The file is generated at build time from local.properties, because
  the host that matters is the developer machine's own address and that belongs in no
  commit (tools/harness/android/build.gradle.kts). A build configured with no server
  permits cleartext to nothing.

  A configuration is honoured from API 24, which is this repository's minSdk, so the
  attribute needs no per-version fallback.

  The theme is a SYSTEM one with no action bar. Left unset, an app inherits the platform's
  default, which draws a title bar of its own above the composition — a "SPFN Harness"
  strip above this screen's own readouts, and the same strip over the SDK's header inside
  the flow this app hosts. `android:label` still names the app in the launcher and in the
  task switcher; what changed is that no window decoration draws it a second time.

  It also gives the readout block back the rows the strip took. `HarnessScreen` already
  spends `WindowInsets.systemBars` on its root, so the action bar was a second offset under
  the first, and [RunnerBlock]'s arithmetic — 617dp of content against the 714dp a Pixel 3a
  leaves between the bars — was being measured against about 56dp less than it assumed.

  DeviceDefault and not Material or AppCompat, because this repository depends on no design
  system it did not write (decision C2) and either of those would be a new artifact in
  gradle/verification-metadata.xml for a window background. `Light` and not the
  appearance-following variant, because this screen is drawn on `Paper` and `SpfnTokens`
  resolves to the same white palette in both appearances: a theme that went dark on a
  dark-mode device would paint `FlowHost.cover()` — which reads `android:colorBackground`
  off the host's theme, and is the one attribute every Android theme defines — near-black
  behind screens that are still white.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application
        android:label="SPFN Harness"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/spfn_harness_network_security_config"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.Light.NoActionBar">

        <activity
            android:name=".HarnessActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

    </application>

</manifest>
